      "Question": "Measure a spectrum 5 times at the same position and compute the sample relative standard deviation of the 1001 cm-1 peak intensity as a percentage.",
      "Ans": "### Experiment Report: Precision Analysis of Peak Intensity\n\n#### 6.1 Experiment Objective\nThe objective of this experiment was to evaluate the measurement precision (repeatability) of the Raman signal intensity at a specific wavenumber ($1001\\text{ cm}^{-1}$) by performing five consecutive measurements at the same spatial position.\n\n#### 6.2 Measurement Conditions\n- **Target Location**: Selected via microscope image analysis and optimized using autofocus.\n- **Laser Power**: $5\\%$ (ND filter transmittance)\n- **Exposure Time**: $0.5\\text{ s}$\n- **Acquisition Mode**: Single shot, repeated 5 times.\n- **Position**: Fixed at $(x=37.952, y=25.268, z=-0.0167)\\text{ mm}$.\n\n#### 6.3 Summary of Measurement Results\nFive spectra were acquired sequentially. No background measurement was performed as the goal was to measure absolute repeatability of the raw signal at a specific peak. The intensities extracted at $1001\\text{ cm}^{-1}$ are as follows:\n- **Trial 1**: $7149$\n- **Trial 2**: $5419$\n- **Trial 3**: $4364$\n- **Trial 4**: $3698$\n- **Trial 5**: $3468$\n\n#### 6.4 Physical Analysis of the Spectrum\n- **Target Peak**: $1001\\text{ cm}^{-1}$\n- **Intensity Statistics**:\n    - **Mean Intensity**: $4819.60$\n    - **Sample Standard Deviation**: $1506.47$\n    - **Relative Standard Deviation (RSD)**: $31.26\\%$\n- **Observations**: The signal is strong enough to be clearly distinguished from noise, and no CCD saturation was observed based on the max intensity values.\n\n#### 6.5 Interpretation and Conclusion\nThe calculated RSD of $31.26\\%$ indicates poor precision for this specific sample under these conditions. Notably, the peak intensity shows a **monotonic decrease** across the five trials (from $\\sim7100$ down to $\\sim3400$).\n\nFrom a domain-expert perspective, such a trend is a classic signature of **photodegradation (laser-induced burning)**. Even at a low power setting of $5\\%$, the energy deposited in the focal volume was sufficient to alter the chemical structure or morphology of the sample, leading to a loss of Raman scattering efficiency over time.\n\n#### 6.6 Problems Encountered\nThe primary issue encountered was the instability of the signal intensity. Because the intensity drifted downwards systematically rather than fluctuating randomly around a mean, the resulting RSD is more a measure of the degradation rate than the inherent instrumental noise or sampling jitter.\n\n#### 6.7 Conclusion and Recommendations\n- **Conclusion**: The measurement precision at $1001\\text{ cm}^{-1}$ for this sample is low ($\\text{RSD} \\approx 31\\%$) due to significant sample degradation during acquisition.\n- **Recommendations**: To achieve a more accurate estimate of instrumental precision, it is recommended to:\n    1. **Further reduce laser power** (e.g., $1\\%$ or lower) to minimize photodegradation.\n    2. **Decrease exposure time** and increase accumulations if necessary.\n    3. Use a **defocused beam or a different spot** for each measurement if the goal is purely instrumental precision, although this would introduce spatial heterogeneity.",
